hadiths[4]{hadithnumber,text}:
"951","It was narrated by Muhammad ibn Abd al-Ala al-San’ani, it was narrated by Khaled, meaning Ibn al-Harith, and Bundar narrated to us, it was Muhammad, they said: Shu’bah narrated to us, Qatada told me, he said: I heard Musa, saying: I asked Ibn Abbas: How can I pray in Mecca if I do not pray in congregation? He said: “Two rak’ahs of the Sunnah of Abu Al-Qasim, may God bless him and grant him peace.” Bundar said, he said: I heard Qatada speaking, on the authority of Musa bin Salamah, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas."
"952","Abu Bakr said: This news, to me, indicates that if the traveler prays with the imam, he must complete the prayer, according to the narration of Laith bin Abi Sulaym, on the authority of Tawoos, on the authority of Ibn Abbas, who, Abu Kurayb told us, Hafs bin Ghayath told us, on the authority of Laith, on the authority of Tawoos: on the authority of Ibn Abbas in Al-Musafer He prays behind the resident. He said: He performs his prayers. We do not rely on the narration of Laith bin Abi Sulaym, except that the report of Qatada, on the authority of Musa bin Salamah, indicates the contradiction of the narration of Sulayman al-Taymi, on the authority of A peacock among the traveler prays behind the resident. He said: If he wishes, he says the salaam in two rak’ahs, and if he wishes, he can go."
"953","He said: Thana. He said: Bundar told us, Yahya told us, on the authority of Shu’bah, on the authority of Sulayman al-Taymi, on the authority of Tawus."
"954","Muhammad bin Yahya told us, Abd al-Samad told us, Shu’bah told us, on the authority of Asim bin Ash-Shabi, that Ibn Umar, “When he was in Mecca, he would pray two rak’ahs.” Two rak'ahs, unless an imam joins him and prays his prayer, then if the imam joins him, he prays his prayer.\""
"
